{d} \\right)\\), we can use the AM-HM (Arithmetic Mean-Harmonic Mean) inequality and the AM-GM (Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean) inequality.\n\nFirst, let's apply the AM-HM inequality to the terms \\(a, b, c, d\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c + d}{4} \\geq \\frac{4}{\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d}}\n\\]\nThis can be rearranged to:\n\\[\n(a + b + c + d) \\left( \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d} \\right) \\geq 16\n\\]\nThis gives us a lower bound of 16 for the expression. To check if this bound can be achieved, we need to see if equality in the AM-HM inequality can be satisfied. Equality in the AM-HM inequality holds if and only if \\(a = b = c = d\\). Let's assume \\(a = b = c = d = k\\). Then the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(a + b + c + d) \\left( \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d} \\right) = (4k) \\left( \\frac{1}{k} + \\frac{1}{k} + \\frac{1}{k} + \\frac{1}{k} \\right) = 4k \\cdot \\frac{4}{k} = 16\n\\]\nThus, the minimum value of the expression is indeed 16, and it is achieved when \\(a = b = c = d\\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is \\(\\boxed{16}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{16}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:135"}
